  "query_preview": "../Friday through Monday/...Ensemble models agreeing \non an upper level ridge dissipating and being replaced by a weak \ntrough over the southern panhandle by Friday, with the ridge \nrebuilding early next week. This shift will bring positive \nvorticity advection and some lifting, while moisture advection \narrives via an easterly wave in the 700mb level. Additionally, a \nsurface maritime ridge forming in the eastern gulf will generate \nnorthwesterly winds across the panhandle which could work in \nconjunction with diurnally driven sea breezes to promote weak \nonshore flow toward the end of the week.\n\nRegarding severe weather potential for end of week/early weekend, \nguidance is keying in on seasonably high CAPE values in parts of \nthe central and southern regions, though forecaster confidence \nremains low due to drier conditions in many of the atmospheric\npressure levels and a lack of support for sustaining storms \nmoving in from Canada. Cumulus fields are more likely than storms,\nas a lack of a cap and significant moisture inhibits potential \nconvection. While westerly flow on Friday might drive some \nlocalized convection through upsloping, marine influences will \nlikely stabilize the surface layer and choke off development. \nMeanwhile, light winds and clearing skies are expected to boost \ntemperatures, with Friday and Saturday continuing this warm spell \nas temperatures reach the upper 70s to low 80s, before cooling \nreturns Sunday into Monday as the surface high pressure weakens. \nThere is still a lot of spread in the track of a low pressure \ncenter moving into the central gulf next week, and if lingering \nhigh pressure will block precipitation from moving in. We will \ncontinue to monitor this next system and associated rainfall \naccumulation.",
